{
    "background_story": "Dewdrop was a majestic stallion with a coat as black as coal. He lived on a rural farm where the owner, a philosophy professor, would often take him on long rides while pondering the ideas of Michel Foucault. The professor would discuss with Dewdrop the concepts of power and knowledge, and how they related to the horse's own experiences. Dewdrop seemed to understand, responding with neighs and whinnies that the professor swore were insightful comments. As they rode, the professor would point out how the farm's social hierarchy, with the farmer at the top and the animals below, was a manifestation of Foucault's ideas on power structures. Dewdrop would snort in apparent agreement, as if recognizing the ways in which his own actions were shaped by the farm's power dynamics. Together, the professor and Dewdrop explored the complexities of Foucault's theories, their rides becoming a unique form of equine-assisted philosophical inquiry.",
